Observed delay is timely and interpretable for airline disruption recovery, but the value of richer operating information depends on whether it changes the recovery decision being supported. This study examines how the incremental decision value of progressively revealed aircraft-linked operating information varies across information representations and recovery decisions. We develop a state-consequence-decision framework linking capacity-constrained screening with local-recovery targeting, and compare Delay-Consequence, Current-History, and Point-Marginal-Joint representations. The data infrastructure spans U.S. airline operations from 2017 to 2022, while primary held-out decision evidence comes from a frozen 2019 chronological evaluation. Delay remains informative at the population level, yet global agreement does not guarantee decision substitutability at the active screening boundary: under the nominal 10\% review capacity, five TURN shortlist positions change and $L^{\mathrm{att}}=0.0724$. History produces limited Stage-I loss but much larger Stage-II loss ($0.0186$ versus $0.9070$). Point compression also causes substantial decision loss, whereas restoring marginal uncertainty recovers most of the observed Point-to-Joint difference; additional Joint alignment adds relatively little. Operational information value is therefore decision specific and boundary dependent, rather than proportional to representational richness.
